<Layout omitEditorialMetadata title="Plan a Website That Works Across Screen Sizes | Holy Webs" description="List what customers need to do: read a service description, find coverage, call, request a quote or book." canonicalUrl="https://holywebs.com/resources/blog/mastering-responsive-design-a-step-by-step-guide/"><div class="migration-page"><header><nav aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/resources/">Resources</a> / <a href="/resources/blog/">Blog</a></nav><h1>Plan a Website That Works Across Screen Sizes</h1></header><article><section><h2>Start with real tasks</h2><p>List what customers need to do: read a service description, find coverage, call, request a quote or book. Use available device data to guide testing, but do not assume a low share of mobile visits proves a design problem.</p></section><section><h2>Let content determine the layout</h2><p>Start with a narrow layout that keeps essential information and actions usable. Add columns or wider navigation only when there is room. Choose layout changes where the content needs them, not from a fixed list of device brands.</p></section><section><h2>Keep the important information available</h2><p>A smaller screen should not hide the service scope or contact path. Menus need clear labels and usable controls. Test long headings, addresses and form errors rather than checking only ideal example content.</p></section><section><h2>Review images and text together</h2><p>Use image formats appropriate to the artwork and supply useful alternative text where needed. Check that photos, video and long text stay within the layout. Vector artwork can scale well, but it is not a replacement for every photograph.</p></section><section><h2>Test the whole customer path</h2><p>Review narrow and wide windows, zoomed text and keyboard navigation. Check menus, focus visibility, field labels and error messages. With authorization, test delivery and booking completion separately: a responsive-looking form does not prove that inquiries arrive.</p></section><section><h2>Keep a repeatable checklist</h2><p>Record the page, viewport, task, observed problem and fix. Recheck affected layouts after content changes. Responsive design is ongoing maintenance, not a guarantee that every future device will work without testing.</p></section><section><h2>Plan your next step</h2><p>Start with your <a href="/resources/blog/defining-your-websites-purpose-and-target-audience/">website purpose and audience</a>, or <a href="/contact/">contact Holy Webs</a> to discuss your priorities.</p></section></article></div>
